{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And they said, etc., and there is no interpreter of it.</b> Meaning, there is no interpreter in reality who could interpret it in any manner whatsoever. And this is the opposite of what is said regarding Pharaoh (Genesis 41:8): ״וְאֵין פּוֹתֵר אוֹתָם״ — meaning, they did interpret, but they did not interpret the dream with an acceptable interpretation." "<b>Do not interpretations belong to Elohim?</b> He means to say that He will interpret it. And he was wise in this, so they would not consider him as aggrandizing himself in the matter, and he said that HaShem has many interpreters of dreams and he is one of them. And this is what he said: ״סַפְּרוּ נָא לִי״." "<b>Further,</b> he intended in saying <b>interpretations</b> in the plural, after the manner of what they said in the chapter HaRo'eh (Berakhot 55a), that twenty-four interpreters of dreams were in Jerusalem, and they all interpreted the dream of Rabbi Bana'ah, and what this one interpreted that one did not interpret, yet all of them were fulfilled — end of quotation. And this is what he said: <b>לֵאלֹהִים פִּתְרוֹנִים</b> — meaning, that many interpretations may be found for the dream, and all of them are correct." "And he was precise in using the word <b>na</b> for two reasons. The first is that the interpretation of a dream is only valid if it is interpreted on its day and not after a delay, for all the matters of the dream are aligned together on its day. And likewise you will find that one does not fast the fast of a dream except on its day, even on Shabbat (Shabbat 11a). Therefore he said <b>סַפְּרוּ נָא לִי</b> — meaning, now, in order to ensure that they not delay even a day or two after the sorrow of their souls has been removed." "<b>And a second reason:</b> after the manner of what they said, of blessed memory (Berakhot 55a), that dreams follow the mouth — and therefore Joseph was concerned that they might precede him and tell their dreams to someone else before him, and the interpretation of that other person would be fulfilled. Therefore he said <b>סַפְּרוּ נָא</b> — meaning: tell me now, in order to give his interpretation precedence. And although they said in the chapter HaRo'eh that twenty-four interpreters of dreams were in Jerusalem, and all of them interpreted the dream of Rabbi Bana'ah as we wrote above, I say that this is specifically so when the interpretations do not contradict one another; but if they did contradict, as you might say: the first one says he will be taken out of prison to greatness and the second says there he will sit until death — only the first is fulfilled. And therefore Joseph said: tell me now, for in this he could trust that his interpretation would be fulfilled."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:47:23.479385560Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}